  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts three polymer-like structural states and links them to function. In the highly ordered case, the brown backbone segments are tightly aligned in parallel rows, forming a dense, regular packing with little visible free space and no obvious side groups between layers. In the disordered case, the same backbones are randomly oriented and mixed with several functional groups shown as pink spheres, blue diamonds, and green triangles, creating a loose, irregular arrangement.\n\nThe rightmost state is a hierarchically ordered structure that appears to combine features of both extremes. The backbones are organized into locally aligned stacked domains separated by gaps, while the functional groups are distributed around and between these domains. A label indicates π–π stacking within the aligned backbone regions, suggesting stronger local ordering than in the disordered sample but more openness than in the fully packed ordered one.\n\nA red arrow labeled heat treatment points from the disordered structure toward the hierarchically ordered one, and the caption below states that this transition increases ion transport and elasticity. Overall, the figure conveys that controlled thermal processing can reorganize a random polymer network into a partially ordered, multi-scale architecture that balances structural order with mobile functional components.",
